  <h2>Licensing and forkability</h2>

  <p>
    The incident renewed a question about what the published source permits.
    On 2 August 2026,
    <a href="/record/sources/notgrubles-license-fork-question/">notgrubles asked</a>
    whether Coinkite's licence would legally prevent forking the firmware to
    provide longer-term support if the company went out of business.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="notgrubles' question about whether the firmware licence would prevent a long-term-support fork if the company folded"
      source="notgrubles, 2 Aug 2026, preserved as an X capture"
      href="/record/sources/notgrubles-license-fork-question/"
      compact
    />
    The
    question sits against an attributed history:
    <a href="/record/sources/gegelsmr4-licensing-history/">a community account posted the same day</a>
    links the firmware's Trezor-derived origins and a later relicensing under
    the Commons Clause to the outside-review question.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="The community account linking the firmware's Trezor-derived origins and Commons Clause relicensing to the outside-review question"
      source="gegelsmr4, 2 Aug 2026, preserved as an X capture"
      href="/record/sources/gegelsmr4-licensing-history/"
      compact
    />
    Neither post is a legal
    analysis, and this archive has not itself construed the licence terms; the
    question is recorded as open.
  </p>

